s independence in 1966.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/moskva-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/moskva-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Moskva River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Moskva River is a river of western Russia, about 470 kilometres long, which rises on the Smolensk–Moscow Upland and flows east through Moscow to join the Oka at…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mosque-of-ibn-tulun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mosque of Ibn Tulun is a congregational mosque in Cairo, built by Ahmad ibn Tulun for his new capital, al-Qata'i, and completed in 879.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba was built as the Great Mosque of the Umayyad capital from 785 under Abd al-Rahman I and enlarged by his successors, notably al-Hakam II…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mosquito-coast.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mosquito Coast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mosquito Coast is the Caribbean lowland of eastern Nicaragua and north-eastern Honduras, home to the Miskito people and Creole and Garífuna communities.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mossi"><span class="enc-name">Mossi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mossi are a people of present-day Burkina Faso, who founded a group of kingdoms in the Volta basin from the fifteenth century.</span></a></li></ul>
